## c1-sketch | Curve sketching | maths | 1 | calc1 | c1-rr
explain 3 | Sketch any curve with four questions
- Where is it zero? (intercepts)
- Where is f′ = 0 or undefined? (critical points: peaks, valleys, flats)
- Where does f″ change sign? (inflection points)
- What happens as x → ±∞ and near any vertical asymptotes?
> f′ tells you rising or falling. f″ tells you the bend

quiz 3 | f(x) = x³ − 3x. Where are its local max and min?
* Max at x = −1, min at x = 1
o Max at x = 1, min at x = −1
o Only a min at x = 0
o No turning points
= f′ = 3x² − 3 = 0 at x = ±1.
= f″ = 6x: negative at −1 (max), positive at 1 (min).
> The second derivative test settles max vs min in one line

explain 2 | Second derivative test
- At a critical point c (f′(c) = 0):
- f″(c) > 0: local minimum. f″(c) < 0: local maximum.
- f″(c) = 0: the test says *nothing*. x⁴, −x⁴ and x³ all have f″(0) = 0.
? If f′(c)=0 and f″(c)=0, what does the second derivative test conclude? :: Nothing. Use the first derivative test instead
> Inflection points are where the bend flips

quiz 3 | Where is the inflection point of f(x) = x³ − 6x² + 5?
* x = 2
o x = 0
o x = 4
o x = 6
= f″ = 6x − 12 = 0 at x = 2, and it changes sign there.
> The bell curve has two inflection points, exactly one standard deviation out

real 4 | The normal distribution bends at ±1σ
- f(x) = e^(−x²/2) has f″ = (x² − 1)e^(−x²/2).
- Zero at x = ±1: the bell switches from concave down to concave up there.
- *That's literally where standard deviation sits on the curve.*
> A cusp is where even the derivative gives up

predict 3 | What does y = x^(2/3) look like at x = 0?
* A sharp cusp pointing down
o A smooth valley
o A corner like |x|
= y′ = (2/3)x^(−1/3) → ±∞ from either side.
= Both sides go vertical at the origin: a cusp.
> Next: using all this to optimise real things

## c1-opt | Optimisation problems | maths | 1 | calc1 | c1-sketch
explain 3 | The optimisation recipe
- 1. Write the thing to maximise as one formula.
- 2. Use the constraint to cut it to one variable.
- 3. Differentiate, set to zero, solve.
- 4. Check endpoints and that it really is a max (or min).
> The classic: the open box

quiz 4 | Cut equal squares of side x from the corners of a 12 × 12 sheet and fold up a box. Which x maximises volume?
* 2
o 3
o 4
o 6
= V = x(12 − 2x)². V′ = (12 − 2x)(12 − 6x) = 0 gives x = 2 or 6.
= x = 6 gives zero volume. x = 2 gives V = 128.
> Drinks cans are an optimisation problem too

predict 4 | For a fixed volume, which cylinder uses the least metal?
* Height equal to diameter
o Height twice the diameter
o Height half the diameter
= Minimise 2πr² + 2πrh with πr²h fixed: you get h = 2r.
= Real cans are taller because the lids are thicker and cost more than the sides.
> Light solves optimisation problems all the time

story 4 | Light takes the fastest path, and that gives you Snell's law
- Fermat (1662): light travels between two points along the path of least time.
- Minimise travel time across two media with speeds v₁ and v₂.
- $ sin θ₁ / v₁ = sin θ₂ / v₂
- *Refraction falls out of a derivative set to zero.*
> A lifeguard running then swimming solves the same problem

quiz 3 | A farmer fences a rectangle against a river (no fence on the river side) with 200 m of fence. Maximum area?
* 5000 m²
o 2500 m²
o 10000 m²
o 4000 m²
= 2x + y = 200, A = x(200 − 2x). A′ = 200 − 4x = 0, x = 50, y = 100.
> Profit is maximised where marginal revenue equals marginal cost

real 3 | Economists optimise with derivatives every day
- Profit P(q) = R(q) − C(q). Set P′(q) = 0.
- So R′(q) = C′(q): *marginal revenue equals marginal cost*.
- "Marginal" just means derivative.
> Next: replacing curves with lines

## c1-lin | Linear approximation | maths | 1 | calc1 | c1-opt
explain 3 | Zoom in far enough and every smooth curve is a line
- Near x = a: f(x) ≈ f(a) + f′(a)(x − a).
- That's the tangent line used as a stand-in for the function.
- It's the first term of a Taylor series.
? Linear approximation of f near a? :: f(x) ≈ f(a) + f′(a)(x − a)
> Estimate square roots in your head

quiz 3 | Use linear approximation to estimate √4.1.
* 2.025
o 2.05
o 2.01
o 2.1
= f(x) = √x, f′(4) = 1/4. √4.1 ≈ 2 + (1/4)(0.1) = 2.025.
= True value: 2.02485…
> Physicists use this constantly: sin θ ≈ θ

explain 3 | The small-angle approximation
- Near 0 (in radians): sin θ ≈ θ, tan θ ≈ θ, cos θ ≈ 1 − θ²/2.
- At 10° (0.1745 rad), sin θ = 0.1736. Less than 1% off.
- *It turns the pendulum equation into simple harmonic motion.*
> Error grows the further you go: the second derivative controls it

predict 3 | Using (1 + x)ⁿ ≈ 1 + nx, what is 1.01¹⁰ roughly?
* 1.1
o 1.01
o 1.21
= 1 + 10(0.01) = 1.10. True value 1.1046.
= This "binomial approximation" appears everywhere in physics.
> Next: L'Hôpital, properly

## c1-lhop | Indeterminate forms | maths | 1 | calc1 | c1-lin
quiz 3 | lim x→0 (1 − cos x)/x² = ?
* 1/2
o 0
o 1
o ∞
= L'Hôpital twice: sin x/(2x) → cos x/2 → 1/2.
= Or use cos x ≈ 1 − x²/2.
> L'Hôpital only works on 0/0 or ∞/∞

myth 3 F | You can use L'Hôpital's rule on any limit of a fraction.
= Only on 0/0 or ∞/∞ forms. Otherwise it gives wrong answers.
= lim x→0 (x + 1)/(x + 2) = 1/2. L'Hôpital would wrongly give 1/1 = 1.
> Exponent forms like 1^∞ need logs first

quiz 4 | lim x→0⁺ xˣ = ?
* 1
o 0
o e
o Undefined
= Take logs: x ln x → 0 (since x beats ln x). So xˣ → e⁰ = 1.
> 0⁰ is an argument mathematicians still have

story 3 | Is 0⁰ equal to 1?
- In combinatorics and power series, 0⁰ = 1 makes every formula work.
- As a limit form, 0⁰ is indeterminate: x⁰ → 1 but 0ˣ → 0.
- Most programming languages return 1. *Context decides.*
> Next: running derivatives backwards

quiz 3 | lim x→∞ (ln x)/√x = ?
* 0
o ∞
o 1
o 1/2
= L'Hôpital: (1/x)/(1/(2√x)) = 2/√x → 0.
= Logs lose to every positive power.
> Next: antiderivatives

## c1-anti | Antiderivatives | maths | 1 | calc1 | c1-lhop
explain 2 | Antiderivatives: undo the derivative
- If F′ = f, then F is an antiderivative of f.
- ∫ xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + C, for n ≠ −1.
- *Always + C*: every constant differentiates to zero.
? ∫ xⁿ dx for n ≠ −1? :: xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + C
> The +C isn't pedantry: it's a whole family of curves

quiz 2 | ∫ (6x² − 4x + 3) dx = ?
* 2x³ − 2x² + 3x + C
o 12x − 4 + C
o 6x³ − 4x² + 3x + C
o 2x³ − 4x² + 3x + C
= Power rule backwards on each term.
> Initial conditions pin down C

quiz 3 | f′(x) = 2x and f(1) = 5. What is f(x)?
* x² + 4
o x² + 5
o 2x² + 3
o x² + C
= f = x² + C. f(1) = 1 + C = 5, so C = 4.
> Physics runs on this: from acceleration back to position

real 3 | Drop a ball: integrate twice
- a = −9.8 m/s². Integrate: v = −9.8t + v₀.
- Integrate again: s = −4.9t² + v₀t + s₀.
- *The two constants are the starting speed and height.*
> Some functions have no antiderivative you can write down

myth 4 F | Every function you can write down has an antiderivative you can write down.
= ∫ e^(−x²) dx, ∫ sin(x)/x dx and ∫ √(1 + x³) dx have no formula in elementary functions.
= Liouville proved this in the 1830s. The antiderivatives exist; they just need new names, like erf(x).
> Next: the definite integral

## c1-def | Definite integrals | maths | 1 | calc1 | c1-anti
explain 3 | The definite integral is signed area
- ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx counts area above the axis as positive, below as negative.
- ∫₀^2π sin x dx = 0: the two humps cancel.
- For total area, integrate |f|.
> Some properties save a lot of work

explain 2 | Integral properties worth knowing
- ∫ₐᵃ f = 0. ∫ₐᵇ f = −∫ᵦₐ f.
- ∫ₐᵇ f + ∫ᵦᶜ f = ∫ₐᶜ f.
- If f ≤ g on [a, b], then ∫ f ≤ ∫ g.
> Average value is an integral too

quiz 3 | Average value of f(x) = x² on [0, 3]?
* 3
o 4.5
o 9
o 1.5
= Average = (1/(b − a))∫ₐᵇ f = (1/3)(27/3) = 3.
> There's a theorem guaranteeing f hits its average

explain 3 | Mean Value Theorem for integrals
- A continuous f on [a, b] actually equals its average value somewhere in the interval.
- $ f(c) = (1/(b − a)) ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx
- Average temperature over a day? At some moment it really was that temperature.
> Next: the Fundamental Theorem in practice

quiz 3 | ∫₀^π sin x dx = ?
* 2
o 0
o 1
o π
= [−cos x]₀^π = −(−1) − (−1) = 2.
> One hump of sine has area exactly 2

## c1-ftc | FTC workout | maths | 1 | calc1 | c1-def
quiz 3 | d/dx ∫₀ˣ cos(t²) dt = ?
* cos(x²)
o sin(x²)
o 2x cos(x²)
o 0
= FTC part 1: differentiating the accumulated area gives back the integrand.
> Variable limits bring in the chain rule

quiz 4 | d/dx ∫₀^(x²) eᵗ dt = ?
* 2x·e^(x²)
o e^(x²)
o eˣ
o 2x·eˣ
= FTC gives e^(x²), times the derivative of the upper limit, 2x.
> A whole new function defined by an integral

explain 4 | ln x can be defined as an integral
- $ ln x = ∫₁ˣ (1/t) dt
- Every property of logs follows from this: ln(ab) = ln a + ln b is just splitting the area.
- *Many textbooks build the logarithm exactly this way.*
> The error function is defined the same way

real 3 | erf(x): an integral with its own name
- erf(x) = (2/√π) ∫₀ˣ e^(−t²) dt. No elementary formula.
- It gives the probability a normal measurement lands within a certain range.
- Calculators and spreadsheets compute it numerically.
> Next: substitution practice

## c1-sub | Substitution practice | maths | 1 | calc1 | c1-ftc
quiz 3 | ∫ x·e^(x²) dx = ?
* ½e^(x²) + C
o e^(x²) + C
o x²e^(x²) + C
o 2e^(x²) + C
= u = x², du = 2x dx. ∫ ½eᵘ du = ½eᵘ.
> Look for an inner function whose derivative is lying around

quiz 3 | ∫ cos x / sin x dx = ?
* ln|sin x| + C
o −ln|sin x| + C
o ln|cos x| + C
o 1/sin² x + C
= u = sin x, du = cos x dx. ∫ du/u = ln|u|.
> Change the limits when you substitute in a definite integral

explain 3 | Definite integrals: change the limits too
- ∫₀¹ 2x(x² + 1)³ dx with u = x² + 1: when x = 0, u = 1; when x = 1, u = 2.
- $ ∫₁² u³ du = [u⁴/4]₁² = 15/4
- Change the limits and you never have to substitute back.
> Sometimes the substitution isn't obvious

quiz 4 | ∫ tan x dx = ?
* −ln|cos x| + C
o ln|cos x| + C
o sec² x + C
o ln|tan x| + C
= tan = sin/cos. u = cos x, du = −sin x dx.
= −ln|cos x| = ln|sec x|.
> Next: areas between curves

## c1-area | Area between curves | maths | 1 | calc1 | c1-sub
explain 2 | Area between curves = top minus bottom
- $ A = ∫ₐᵇ (top − bottom) dx
- Find where the curves cross: those are often your limits.
- If they swap places, split the integral.
> Sometimes integrating in y is easier

quiz 3 | Area between y = x and y = x² on [0, 1]?
* 1/6
o 1/2
o 1/3
o 1/12
= x is on top. ∫₀¹ (x − x²) dx = 1/2 − 1/3 = 1/6.
> Area also gives you a famous inequality

quiz 4 | Area enclosed by y = 4 − x² and the x-axis?
* 32/3
o 16/3
o 8
o 16
= Crosses at ±2. ∫₋₂² (4 − x²) dx = 16 − 16/3 = 32/3.
= Archimedes' rule: 4/3 of the inscribed triangle (area 8).
> Next: volumes by spinning

## c1-vol | Volumes of revolution | maths | 1 | calc1 | c1-area
explain 3 | Disc method: slice it like salami
- Spin y = f(x) around the x-axis. Each slice is a disc of area πf(x)².
- $ V = π ∫ₐᵇ f(x)² dx
- Hollow middle? Subtract the inner disc: washers.
> Derive the volume of a sphere in one line

quiz 4 | Spin y = √(r² − x²) around the x-axis. Volume?
* (4/3)πr³
o πr³
o (2/3)πr³
o 4πr²
= π∫₋ᵣʳ (r² − x²) dx = π(2r³ − 2r³/3) = (4/3)πr³.
> Archimedes wanted this result on his tomb

story 4 | Archimedes' tombstone had a sphere in a cylinder
- He proved a sphere has 2/3 the volume of the cylinder that wraps it, and 2/3 of its surface area too.
- He asked for that diagram on his grave.
- Cicero found the overgrown tomb in Syracuse around 75 BC, recognising it by the sphere and cylinder.
> Shells: another way to slice

explain 3 | Shell method: slice into tubes
- Spin around the y-axis. Each thin vertical strip makes a cylindrical shell.
- $ V = 2π ∫ₐᵇ x·f(x) dx
- Use whichever method gives the easier integral.
> Next: making calculus rigorous

## c1-ra1 | Analysis: sequences and completeness | maths | 3 | calc1 | c1-vol
explain 3 | What it means for a sequence to converge
- aₙ → L if for every ε > 0, eventually all terms are within ε of L.
- "Eventually" = there's an N after which it holds.
- 1/n → 0: for any ε, pick N > 1/ε.
? Definition of aₙ → L? :: For every ε > 0 there is N such that n > N implies |aₙ − L| < ε
> The rationals have holes. The reals don't

explain 4 | Completeness: why calculus needs the real numbers
- The sequence 1, 1.4, 1.41, 1.414, … is all rational, but its limit √2 isn't.
- The reals fill every hole: any bounded increasing sequence converges.
- *Without completeness, the Intermediate Value Theorem fails.* x² − 2 would skip zero on the rationals.
> The least upper bound property is the engine

quiz 3 | What is the supremum (least upper bound) of {1 − 1/n : n ≥ 1}?
* 1
o 0.99
o It doesn't exist
o 0
= Every term is below 1 and gets arbitrarily close. 1 itself is never reached.
= Supremum ≠ maximum: this set has no maximum.
> Cauchy found a way to test convergence without knowing the limit

explain 4 | Cauchy sequences converge without naming the limit
- Terms get arbitrarily close to *each other*: |aₘ − aₙ| < ε for large m, n.
- In the reals, Cauchy ⇔ convergent.
- In the rationals, a Cauchy sequence can head towards a hole. That's another way to define completeness.
> Every bounded sequence has a convergent subsequence

explain 4 | Bolzano–Weierstrass
- Every bounded sequence of reals has a convergent subsequence.
- Proof idea: keep halving the interval, always keeping a half with infinitely many terms.
- It's the workhorse behind the Extreme Value Theorem.
? Bolzano–Weierstrass theorem? :: Every bounded real sequence has a convergent subsequence
> Next: continuity, rigorously

myth 3 F | A sequence that keeps increasing must go to infinity.
= 1 − 1/n increases forever but converges to 1.
= Monotone convergence theorem: increasing and bounded ⇒ converges.
> Next: uniform continuity

## c1-ra2 | Analysis: continuity rigorously | maths | 4 | calc1 | c1-ra1
explain 4 | Uniform continuity: one δ for everywhere
- Continuous: for each point and each ε there's a δ (which may depend on the point).
- Uniformly continuous: one δ works across the whole domain.
- x² on all reals isn't uniform: it gets steeper, so δ must shrink as x grows.
> Heine–Cantor: on closed bounded intervals, the two coincide

quiz 4 | Which is uniformly continuous on (0, 1)?
* √x
o 1/x
o sin(1/x)
o 1/x²
= √x extends continuously to [0, 1], a compact interval, so it's uniformly continuous.
= 1/x and sin(1/x) oscillate or blow up near 0.
> Uniform convergence is the property that lets you swap limits

explain 4 | Pointwise vs uniform convergence
- fₙ(x) = xⁿ on [0, 1] converges pointwise to 0 (for x < 1) and 1 (at x = 1): a discontinuous limit.
- Each fₙ is continuous. The limit isn't.
- *Uniform convergence preserves continuity.* Pointwise convergence doesn't.
? Why does uniform convergence matter? :: It preserves continuity and allows swapping limits with integrals
> This is exactly why Fourier series misbehave at jumps

story 4 | Cauchy published a wrong theorem
- In 1821 Cauchy claimed a convergent sum of continuous functions is always continuous.
- Fourier series of square waves are counterexamples: continuous sines summing to a jump.
- The fix, uniform convergence, took decades to be stated clearly. *Even the founders of rigour slipped.*
> Calculus 1 is done. Calculus 2 builds integration into an art
